WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.780
Why does earliest-finish interval scheduling

2
00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:05.060
work? We will choose the

3
00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:06.900
largest compatible subset of jobs

4
00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:09.620
on one resource, trace every

5
00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:11.960
acceptance and rejection, and justify

6
00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:13.200
the greedy rule by an

7
00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:14.180
exchange argument.

8
00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:16.000
Represent a job as an

9
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.200
interval including its start but

10
00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:20.980
excluding its finish. Two jobs

11
00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:22.220
that meet at an endpoint

12
00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:24.840
are therefore compatible, because the

13
00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:26.680
first has finished exactly when

14
00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:27.420
the second begins.

15
00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:29.620
The rule is to sort

16
00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:31.640
jobs by increasing finish time.

17
00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:33.660
Scan that order and accept

18
00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:35.200
a job exactly when its

19
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:36.440
start is at least the

20
00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:38.020
finish of the last accepted

21
00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:38.380
job.

22
00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:40.720
Our five jobs are A

23
00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:42.660
from zero to three, B

24
00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:44.380
from one to two, C

25
00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:46.140
from two to four, D

26
00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:47.440
from three to five, and

27
00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:48.740
E from four to six.

28
00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:50.760
Their finish order is B,

29
00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:53.680
A, C, D, E, so

30
00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:55.220
the scan begins with B.

31
00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:58.080
Accept B, which occupies the

32
00:00:58.080 --> 00:00:59.720
resource from one to two.

33
00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:02.000
Set the last accepted finish

34
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:03.720
to two; A then starts

35
00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:06.000
at zero and conflicts, so

36
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.040
reject A without changing that

37
00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:08.720
finish marker.

38
00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:11.680
C starts at two, exactly

39
00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:13.840
the marker, so accept C

40
00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:15.060
and move the marker to

41
00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:17.200
four. D starts at three

42
00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:18.860
and conflicts, so it is

43
00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:20.420
rejected while the marker stays

44
00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:21.020
at four.

45
00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:24.400
E starts at four, so

46
00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:26.480
accept it after C. The

47
00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:28.620
result B, C, E contains

48
00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:30.480
three jobs, and its touching

49
00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:32.340
endpoints are valid under our

50
00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:33.280
interval convention.

51
00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:36.000
To prove the rule, consider

52
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.080
an optimal solution agreeing with

53
00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:39.900
greedy for the longest possible

54
00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:42.080
prefix. At the first difference,

55
00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:44.220
greedy chooses a compatible job

56
00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:45.920
that finishes no later than

57
00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:47.640
the optimal solution's next job.

58
00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:49.980
Replace that optimal job by

59
00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:52.520
greedy's choice. It remains compatible

60
00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:54.420
with the common prefix, and

61
00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:55.900
because it finishes no later,

62
00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:57.600
every later job from the

63
00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:59.240
optimal schedule still fits.

64
00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:02.340
The replacement preserves optimality and

65
00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:05.340
extends the agreeing prefix, contradicting

66
00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:07.920
its maximal choice. Repeating this

67
00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.500
reasoning establishes that earliest-finish greedy

68
00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:13.480
attains a maximum-cardinality compatible subset.

69
00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:15.440
If greedy stopped with another

70
00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:17.880
optimal job still compatible, its

71
00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:19.140
scan would have accepted another

72
00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:21.100
job, so it cannot terminate

73
00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:22.880
earlier than that agreeing optimum.

74
00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:25.740
Choosing the smallest start time

75
00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:27.840
is not interchangeable: a job

76
00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:29.200
from zero to ten can

77
00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:30.600
block three jobs from one

78
00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:32.100
to two, two to three,

79
00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:34.940
and three to four. Shortest

80
00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:36.700
duration also fails, because the

81
00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:37.920
job from three to five

82
00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:39.600
blocks both zero to four

83
00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:40.640
and four to eight.

84
00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:43.060
Sorting costs big O of

85
00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:44.480
n log n, and the

86
00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:46.400
scan costs big O of

87
00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:47.900
n, or linear total if

88
00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:49.460
finish order is already given.

89
00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:51.820
Only accepted jobs update the

90
00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:53.700
marker, and its initial value

91
00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:55.140
must be a valid lower

92
00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:57.300
bound on starts, rather than

93
00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:59.920
blindly zero when negative times

94
00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:00.580
are allowed.

95
00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:02.380
Things to take note of:

96
00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:04.860
this is unweighted subset selection

97
00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:06.640
on one resource, and changing

98
00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:08.240
the objective can invalidate the

99
00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:11.020
proof. Interval partitioning schedules every

100
00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:12.600
job using the fewest resources

101
00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:13.900
and is excluded in the

102
00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:15.620
supplied review, so do not

103
00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:17.080
answer a different problem just

104
00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:18.620
because its name sounds familiar.
